(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-08":{"number":8,"title":"A Walk Along the Wall","content":"Iroh took me walking along the wall because he believed cities should be seen from the places built to protect them.\n\n“That is a poetic way to say we are climbing stairs,” I said.\n\n“I am a poetic man.”\n\n“You are a man who carries snacks in his sleeves.”\n\n“A poetic man with priorities.”\n\nThe wall rose above the city, broad enough for two carts to pass. Wind moved along the stone. Below, Ba Sing Se unfolded in layers: markets, gardens, tiled roofs, narrow alleys, and the distant palace shining beneath the morning sun.\n\nIroh walked beside me with a small basket over one arm. He had packed tea, fruit, and a blanket. I suspected he could turn any errand into a picnic if given enough time.\n\n“Do you ever get tired of looking at the city?” I asked.\n\n“No. It changes even when it appears still.”\n\n“You say that about people too.”\n\n“People change more dramatically. They simply hide it better.”\n\nNear the parapet, we passed two laborers laying fresh mortar over a cracked section of stone. One recognized Iroh and straightened so abruptly that the bucket in his hands tipped.\n\n“General.”\n\nThe old title landed between us like something dropped from the height of the wall. Iroh looked at the bucket instead of the man.\n\n“Your work is more important than my interruption,” he said. “Please, don't spill the mortar on my account.”\n\nThe laborer laughed uncertainly. “They say you know these walls better than the surveyors.”\n\n“I knew how to approach them,” Iroh replied. “That is not the same as knowing whether they are sound.”\n\nThe second worker held up a measuring cord. Its knots were dark with rain. He explained that the gap had widened after a winter freeze and that the city had approved only the outer patch, not an inspection of the stone beneath. Iroh crouched to look, careful to keep his hands off the work.\n\n“You could ask the survey office to return,” he said.\n\n“They've already said the money is spent.”\n\nI recognized the expression on his face. It was the one he wore when the old maps in the tea shop mentioned a street he'd once commanded men to cross. He wanted a solution. He wanted it before anyone could ask how much it would cost.\n\n“What do you need us to do?” I asked the workers.\n\n“Tell the office we can't guarantee the repair without looking underneath,” the older man said. “Put it in writing. They listen when a famous person signs.”\n\nIroh took the folded document but didn't sign. “I will support an inspection request in your names. I would not want my title to turn your evidence into an old soldier's opinion.”\n\nThe workers exchanged a look. The man with the cord nodded. The request would still be difficult; at least the people who had measured the crack would be allowed to say what they found.\n\nWhen we went on, the city felt different beneath my feet. I had climbed for a view. He had climbed a structure his younger self had wanted to defeat. Neither of us could make that history picturesque by unpacking a picnic.\n\n\nI stopped near a watchtower. Below, a group of children chased one another through a courtyard. Their laughter rose on the wind.\n\n“When I first arrived, I thought this city would swallow me,” I said.\n\n“It has a large appetite.”\n\n“I didn’t know where anything was. I didn’t know who to trust. I kept expecting someone to ask what I was doing here.”\n\n“Did they?”\n\n“You did.”\n\nIroh considered. “I asked what you were seeking.”\n\n“That was worse.”\n\n“Questions can be doors.”\n\n“They can also be traps.”\n\nHe nodded. “That is why the person asking must be willing to accept the answer.”\n\nI leaned against the stone railing. The wind pulled at my clothes.\n\n“What answer were you hoping for?” he asked.\n\n“I didn’t know.”\n\n“And now?”\n\nI looked at the Jasmine Dragon's green sign far below. It was tiny from the wall, but visible.\n\n“A place where I don’t have to explain why I need to rest.”\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n“A place where I can be useful without being used,” I continued. “A place where people can want me without needing to own me.”\n\n“These are good things to seek.”\n\n“Are they?”\n\n“They are necessary things.”\n\nHe unwrapped the blanket beneath the tower. We sat together with the basket between us. Iroh poured tea into two cups. The blend was light and green, with a faint citrus scent.\n\n“What is this?”\n\n“A tea for clear weather.”\n\n“The sky is clear.”\n\n“The sky is not the only weather.”\n\nI took a sip. The flavor was bright enough to make me sit straighter.\n\nIroh passed me a slice of pear.\n\n“You feed me whenever I become emotional,” I said.\n\n“Food helps the body remember it is alive.”\n\n“And tea?”\n\n“Tea helps the heart slow down enough to notice.”\n\nI looked at him. “Did you learn that before or after the war?”\n\n“During.”\n\nThe answer carried no ornament.\n\nIroh looked over the wall. “In the army, every day was loud. Orders, metal, horses, fire. I learned to make tea in a small pot because the act of pouring water gave my hands something gentle to do.”\n\n“Did it help?”\n\n“Not at first.”\n\n“What changed?”\n\n“I stopped asking it to erase anything.”\n\nI turned the cup in my hands.\n\n“I want to stop asking you to erase things,” I said.\n\nIroh looked at me. “You have not asked.”\n\n“Not with words.”\n\n“Then let us be precise. I am not a cure for your past. You are not a cure for mine.”\n\n“That sounds very romantic.”\n\n“I am a man of many talents.”\n\n“You’re an emotional physician.”\n\n“I prefer tea master.”\n\nI laughed. He touched the back of my hand with two fingers.\n\n“You may want me,” he said. “You may love me. You may sit beside me while I remember painful things. But neither of us should make the other responsible for becoming whole.”\n\nThe wind moved around the tower.\n\n“What if I want you anyway?”\n\n“Then want me.”\n\n“That easy?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You have a talent for making difficult things sound simple.”\n\n“I am making progress,” he said. “Once, I would have tried to win this conversation. Now I offer you the pear.”\n\nHe offered me another slice of pear.\n\nI took it, then turned my hand beneath his. My fingers linked.\n\n“I want you,” I said.\n\nIroh's breath caught.\n\n“I know,” he replied.\n\n“You’re supposed to say it back.”\n\n“I want you too.”\n\nThe words were quiet but unmistakable.\n\nI leaned toward him. The kiss took place with the city spread below me, with wind in my hair and tea cooling beside my knee.\n\nIroh kissed slowly, as though he had all the time in the world. His hand rested at the side of my neck. When I moved closer, his thumb brushed beneath my ear.\n\n“Still comfortable?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you wish to continue?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then we should return to the shop before I forget the tea kettle.”\n\nI pulled back. “You are blaming the kettle?”\n\n“It has poor timing.”\n\n“You’re the one who brought me to the wall.”\n\n“The wall is very persuasive.”\n\nI kissed him once more, then packed the blanket.\n\nOn the walk down, his hand stayed in mine. The city below seemed less like a maze. I still did not know every road. I still did not know what the future would ask.\n\nBut I knew where the Jasmine Dragon was.\n\nAnd I knew who would be listening for my footsteps.\n\nThe walk along the wall became something I repeated even after returning to the shop. On busy days, I and Iroh climbed before opening. On quiet days, I climbed after closing and carried tea in a flask.\n\nThe city looked different from above depending on the weather. In bright sun, the roofs formed a sea of green and gold. In rain, the streets became ribbons of silver. At night, the lanterns made the city seem less like a fortress and more like a constellation someone had built by hand.\n\nOne morning, Iroh took me to a section of the wall where several stones had been replaced. The new blocks were pale against the old ones.\n\n“This is where the siege damaged it,” he said.\n\n“Were you here?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe rested his hand on one of the old stones.\n\n“I stood on the other side,” he continued. “I believed the wall was an obstacle between my army and victory.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I see a structure built by people who wanted to protect a home.”\n\nI touched the new stone. “Can a wall be both protection and prison?”\n\n“Most things can be both. It depends on whether there is a door.”\n\n“And who has the key.”\n\nIroh glanced at me. “Exactly.”\n\nI took the key to the tea shop from beneath my shirt. It rested warm against my skin.\n\n“You gave me one,” I said.\n\n“I did.”\n\n“That does not make the shop mine.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“But it means I can enter.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And leave.”\n\n“Always.”\n\nThe certainty eased me.\n\n“You are not afraid I will use the key to disappear?” I asked.\n\n“I would be sad if you did.”\n\n“That is not the same as afraid.”\n\n“I am afraid of many things. I do not want fear to choose our locks.”\n\nI leaned my back against the wall. Iroh stood beside me.\n\n“You are very different here,” he said.\n\n“Here?”\n\n“Above the city. You watch every road.”\n\n“You watch roads too.”\n\n“I have had more years to disguise it.”\n\nI looked over the edge. “I am afraid of being trapped.”\n\n“By the city?”\n\n“By any place that begins to feel like home.”\n\nIroh waited.\n\n“If I care about it, someone can use it against me,” I said. “If I need it, leaving becomes a loss instead of a choice.”\n\nHe nodded slowly. “Then home must include the ability to leave.”\n\n“That sounds contradictory.”\n\n“It is a difficult balance. A bird may rest on a branch because it knows it can fly.”\n\n“You’re comparing me to a bird.”\n\n“A very determined bird.”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“I am more of a turtleduck.”\n\nI laughed. He looked pleased.\n\nAt the base of the wall, he bought two cups of tea from a street vendor. The vendor recognized him but did not say the title.\n\n“You are becoming anonymous,” I said.\n\n“It is peaceful.”\n\n“Do you miss being known?”\n\n“Sometimes. But being known and being watched are different things.”\n\nI drank the tea. “I know that.”\n\nHe took my cup when I finished and carried both.\n\n“I can carry my own,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are going to say that again.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Then I am going to say I want you to carry them.”\n\nIroh looked at me. “Very well.”\n\nHe walked with both cups in one hand and took my other hand in his.\n\nThe city gate opened ahead.\n\nI had a key, a road, and the right to leave.\n\nI entered the tea shop anyway.\n\nIroh held the door for me.\n\n“You are becoming predictable,” he said.\n\n“Only in the best ways.”\n\nHe smiled as if I had given the correct answer.","word_count":1884},"avatar-18":{"number":18,"title":"The Shop After Closing","content":"The tea shop after closing had a different kind of silence.\n\nDuring the day, every sound belonged to someone: cups touching saucers, chairs moving across the floor, customers laughing, the kettle announcing that it had been ignored. At night, the quiet belonged to the wood, the walls, the jasmine plant, and the two of us.\n\nIroh began locking the door earlier.\n\n“You are becoming suspicious,” I said.\n\n“I am becoming efficient.”\n\n“You closed thirty minutes early.”\n\n“The chairs were tired.”\n\n“The chairs have no feelings.”\n\n“You have met very few chairs.”\n\nMina was still there, stacking the last stools. She had a basket on her arm and the unmistakable expression of a woman who had overheard two people being romantic at the expense of a business.\n\n“You cannot keep closing early without changing the posted hours,” she told Iroh. “The delivery boy came back for his pay, and the back door was locked.”\n\nI put the tin down.\n\n“I thought the day's work was finished,” Iroh said.\n\n“His wasn't. Neither was mine.”\n\nThere was no anger in her voice, which made it harder to dismiss. She showed him the wage sheet, with her closing time written correctly and the boy's final half-hour missing. The kettle was cold. Beyond the shutters, the street vendors were packing away their baskets.\n\nIroh read the line twice. “An oversight.”\n\n“Yes,” Mina said. “Fix it before you make the next one.”\n\nHe did. He counted the coins into a separate envelope, wrote a note for the runner, and asked Mina whether she wanted the revised closing shift paid or moved. She chose to be paid. She also wanted the sign on the door to match the hours people could rely on.\n\nAfter she left, Iroh looked at the envelope in his palm.\n\n“You were right,” I said.\n\n“An observation I am beginning to dislike.”\n\n“About the chairs being tired?”\n\n“About people having work that does not end when I am ready for a quiet evening.”\n\nHe replaced the closing notice himself, even though the new hours meant we would have fewer minutes alone. I watched him do it. I was attracted to him at the most inconvenient moments, especially when he was trying not to make an apology into a performance.\n\n\nI stood behind the counter, sorting tea leaves into small tins. Iroh moved through the shop with a candle in one hand. The light caught the silver in his hair.\n\n“Are you going to help?” I asked.\n\n“I am supervising.”\n\n“You are watching me.”\n\n“That is part of supervision.”\n\n“You have been watching me for five minutes.”\n\n“A thorough supervisor is a good supervisor.”\n\nI put the tin down. “Come here.”\n\nIroh's eyebrows lifted. “That sounded like an order.”\n\n“You can refuse.”\n\n“I could.”\n\n“Are you going to?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe crossed the room. I met him beside the counter. His hands settled at my waist.\n\n“Still comfortable?” he asked.\n\n“You are becoming predictable.”\n\n“I am becoming careful.”\n\n“You can be both.”\n\nHe kissed me.\n\nThe kiss was gentle and unhurried. Iroh paused to check that I was comfortable, and I told him I was. The shop around us had gone quiet.\n\n“That was not very scholarly,” I murmured.\n\n“I have studied many subjects.”\n\n“Was kissing one of them?”\n\n“I am conducting research.”\n\n“The experiment is going well.”\n\n“I am pleased.”\n\nHe kissed my cheek, then asked if I wanted to go upstairs. I said yes, and we walked there together.\n\nThe candle flame bent in the draft. Outside, a cart rolled past, its driver singing under his breath.\n\n“We should go upstairs,” Iroh said.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because the shop has windows.”\n\n“You are concerned about being seen?”\n\n“I am concerned about giving the neighborhood an unnecessarily interesting morning.”\n\nI laughed. He kissed me again, longer this time.\n\n“You know,” I said, “you could just say you want privacy.”\n\n“I want privacy.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe took my hand. We locked the back door together. The stairs creaked beneath me.\n\nUpstairs, the room smelled of jasmine and rain. The balcony doors were open. Iroh closed them, then turned to me.\n\n“We should talk,” he said.\n\n“That sounds ominous.”\n\n“It is about the future.”\n\n“The future is always ominous when someone says it like that.”\n\nHe took off his outer coat and draped it over the chair.\n\n“You may not wish to remain above the shop forever,” he said. “You may want a place entirely your own.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You may decide the work is not what you want.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You may decide you do not want me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe looked at me. “I am telling you because I do not want tonight to become a promise you feel required to keep.”\n\nThe tenderness of it made my chest ache.\n\n“I want tonight,” I said.\n\n“That is enough.”\n\n“And I want tomorrow.”\n\n“That is also enough.”\n\n“And I want to see what happens after tomorrow.”\n\nIroh stepped closer. “Then we will see it.”\n\nI reached for him. “Can I kiss you?”\n\nHis smile was warm. “You may.”\n\nWe talked about the future, then shared another kiss. When we were ready to be alone, we closed the door together and left the rest of the evening private.\n\nWe checked in with one another, agreed that we both wanted to stay, and let the evening slow down. The room grew quiet around us; the rest of the night was ours.\n\nIn the morning, I found Iroh asleep beside me, one hand open on the blanket between us. I placed my hand in it.\n\nHis fingers closed around mine without waking.\n\nThe tea shop below would open soon.\n\nFor a little longer, I let the world wait.\n\nWhen I woke, Iroh was already watching the dawn through the balcony doors.\n\n“You are awake,” he said.\n\n“You are staring.”\n\n“I am observing.”\n\n“That is what you call staring.”\n\n“It is a more respectable word.”\n\nI sat up. His outer robe was draped over the chair. The room smelled of jasmine and rain.\n\n“Did you sleep?” I asked.\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“You are using my answer.”\n\n“It is a useful one.”\n\nHe brought me tea in the cup with my name. I accepted it.\n\n“The shop opens in an hour,” I said.\n\n“Then we should not be here.”\n\n“You are the owner.”\n\n“A poor excuse for lateness.”\n\n“You have been late before.”\n\n“I was younger.”\n\n“You are using age again.”\n\n“It remains effective.”\n\nI drank. The tea was perfect.\n\n“I want to ask about work,” I said.\n\nIroh sat at the edge of the bed. “Ask.”\n\n“Do you still want me as an employee?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Even if we are together?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What if one of us has a bad day?”\n\n“We do not punish the other with silence.”\n\n“What if we argue?”\n\n“We step away from the customers. We do not use wages or housing as weapons.”\n\n“What if we break up?”\n\nThe question changed the room.\n\nIroh looked at me for a long moment. “Then we make a plan that protects your home and your work. You will not lose the room because a relationship ended.”\n\nMy eyes burned.\n\n“You mean that?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Even if you are hurt?”\n\n“Especially then.”\n\nThe answer was not romantic in the way songs were romantic. It was better. It gave love a structure strong enough to hold pain without turning it into punishment.\n\nI reached for him. He took my hand.\n\n“I do not plan to break up with you,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But a plan should include difficult possibilities.”\n\n“You are very responsible.”\n\n“It is a burden.”\n\n“You can set it down sometimes.”\n\n“I am trying.”\n\nI kissed him. He smiled against my mouth.\n\nThe shop bell rang downstairs.\n\n“We are late,” I said.\n\n“The customers may survive.”\n\n“The kettle will not.”\n\n“The kettle is immortal.”\n\nWe dressed together, bumping shoulders in the narrow room. Iroh tied his sash while I searched for the key to the shop.\n\n“It is in your pocket,” he said.\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“I am observing.”\n\nI found it in his pocket instead.\n\n“You stole my key.”\n\n“I was keeping it safe.”\n\n“From what?”\n\n“Your forgetfulness.”\n\nI took it back and kissed him again.\n\nDownstairs, the first customer pretended not to notice that my hair was untidy and Iroh's robe was tied incorrectly.\n\nThe shop opened.\n\nThe day began.\n\nThat night, I made a new rule: one evening each week belonged to neither the shop nor the road. It belonged to me.\n\nIroh agreed.\n\nThen he asked whether tea counted as work.\n\nI told him it depended on whether he was making it for customers or for me.\n\nHe smiled. “Then I know what to brew.”\n\nThe first evening belonging only to me began with a walk to the river. Iroh brought no notebook, no letters, and no map. I carried a small basket of food.\n\n“You have planned,” he said.\n\n“I have planned not to plan.”\n\n“An advanced discipline.”\n\nI sat beneath a tree. The river reflected the lanterns from the bridge. Iroh poured tea from a flask.\n\n“Is this shop tea?” I asked.\n\n“It is personal tea.”\n\n“What is the difference?”\n\n“The shop tea is measured. The personal tea is made by feeling.”\n\nI tasted it. It was softer than his usual blend, with honey and citrus.\n\n“It is good,” I said.\n\n“You sound surprised.”\n\n“You made it by feeling.”\n\n“My feelings are refined.”\n\n“Your feelings are dramatic.”\n\n“Refined drama.”\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n“What do you want to do when there is nothing to fix?” I asked.\n\nIroh thought. “Sit with you.”\n\n“That is all?”\n\n“Some days, yes.”\n\n“You could play pai sho.”\n\n“You would lose.”\n\n“I could throw a tile.”\n\n“A powerful strategy.”\n\nI laughed. The evening passed without a crisis. It felt like a rare kind of magic.\n\nWhen I returned to the shop, Iroh opened the door and held it for me.\n\n“You first,” he said.\n\n“You are becoming polite.”\n\n“I am becoming romantic.”\n\n“You have always been romantic.”\n\n“I have been hiding it beneath tea.”\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nHe kissed me in the doorway.\n\nThe kiss lasted until the river lanterns blurred behind my closed eyes.\n\nWhen I finally separated, Iroh kept his forehead against mine and asked whether tomorrow could belong to the shop again.\n\n“Only if tonight belongs to us,” I said.\n\n“It does.”\n\nI walked home beneath the lanterns with my hands joined.\n\nMina's corrected closing notice hung beside the shop door. Iroh checked the latch once, then left the key in my hand while he took the little wage envelope to the morning dispatch shelf. We would have an ordinary day tomorrow. I wanted that, too.","word_count":1777},"avatar-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Price of the Mountain Mint","content":"The supplier's letter smelled of rain and spoiled leaves.\n\nIroh read it at the back counter, his expression growing more serious with every line.\n\n\"Bad news?\" I asked.\n\n\"The mountain harvest was poor. Too much rain at the wrong time.\"\n\nHe handed me the note.\n\nThe supplier had lost nearly half the expected mint crop. Transporting what remained would cost more, and the farmers had asked their regular buyers to take smaller orders rather than forcing prices low enough to leave families without income.\n\n\"They're trying to share the loss,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Can we manage with less?\"\n\nIroh looked toward the tea tins.\n\n\"We can. But not if I insist on serving everything as though nothing has changed.\"\n\nHe sounded unhappy about it.\n\nThe Jasmine Dragon had already lost stock in the roof leak. We needed another shipment, but the price was now nearly twice what we'd planned. The bakery was feeling the same shortage in some of its herbs. At the clinic, Katara's volunteers had been rationing mint for certain medicinal preparations.\n\nNone of this could be solved by wishing a more generous harvest into existence.\n\nWe called a meeting with the people who relied on the shipments.\n\nMara arrived with flour on her sleeve. Tamsin brought a pencil and the expression of somebody prepared to object to numbers that didn't add up. Neri came with the route schedule. Aang had sent a note from Riverstone asking us to prioritize the clinic's confirmed medicinal needs.\n\nIroh poured a simpler tea than usual.\n\nNobody complained.\n\n\"We can divide what remains,\" he said, \"but I will not ask the growers to sell at a loss.\"\n\nMara nodded.\n\n\"Good. They're people, not a cupboard.\"\n\nThe figures were less encouraging.\n\nThe clinic needed a guaranteed supply. The bakery could change two recipes. The teahouse could reduce its premium blend, but enough customers depended on the ordinary tea for a small comfort during their working day that raising every price would hurt people who had little to spare.\n\n\"Then don't raise every price,\" I said.\n\nTamsin lifted an eyebrow.\n\n\"Where does the money come from?\"\n\n\"Let's find out before we guess.\"\n\nWe listed the costs on one sheet: tea, fuel, clean water, staff time, transport, cup repairs, and an emergency reserve. Iroh watched me write.\n\n\"You're forgetting something,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Payment for the growers' risk.\"\n\nI added it.\n\nThe numbers became uglier.\n\nMara smiled.\n\n\"At least they're honest.\"\n\nA good part of the morning went into arguing over what fair meant. Iroh wanted everyone to have the best tea he could serve. Tamsin wanted the business to survive. Neri wanted the delivery route kept open. I wanted us to stop talking as though these desires were enemies.\n\nIn the end, we designed three offerings.\n\nThe ordinary house blend would remain affordable. It used local herbs and roasted grain with a little citrus peel, warm and pleasant without pretending to be rare mountain mint. A smaller premium service would cost more and use the limited mint supply, with the price clearly explained. The clinic's confirmed allotment would be reserved before any tea was sold.\n\n\"No free miracle,\" I said.\n\n\"No,\" Iroh agreed. \"Just careful work.\"\n\nWe posted the menu that afternoon.\n\nThe first customer read it, frowned, and asked why the famous mountain mint tea had become so expensive.\n\nIroh explained the harvest without turning the farmers into a tragedy for entertainment.\n\n\"Then what is that one?\" the customer asked, pointing to the house blend.\n\n\"A new recipe,\" Iroh said. \"It may disappoint you.\"\n\nThe customer sniffed the cup.\n\n\"Smells good.\"\n\n\"It is intended to.\"\n\nThey tasted it.\n\n\"Hm.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"I'll have another.\"\n\nIroh's eyebrows rose.\n\nWe both tried not to look relieved.\n\nThe second customer complained that a famous shop shouldn't serve a cheaper alternative. The third asked if the house blend came in a travel flask. By evening, we'd sold more of it than we'd expected.\n\nNot everyone was pleased. That was normal. A business could make a fair decision and still disappoint someone.\n\nThe dispute wasn't over the price of mint. It was over a single line on the supplier's receipt: *Carriage cost to be absorbed by buyer.*\n\n“What does that mean?” Mara asked.\n\n“It means the merchant wants us to pay the additional cart fee,” Iroh said.\n\n“And if we don't?”\n\n“Then the growers may have to.”\n\nMina took the invoice from him. “Or the merchant is charging us the old fee plus a second one and hoping no one asks.”\n\nFor a moment Iroh looked offended on behalf of the whole system of trade. Then he smiled without much pleasure.\n\n“That,” he said, “is why I prefer a good ledger to a convincing speech.”\n\nWe sent a message asking the growers to confirm what they had agreed to and to whom they had already paid. The answer came back by evening: the carrier had raised his fee once, not twice. The extra line was an error made by a new clerk in the warehouse. We could settle the genuine cost without taking it out of anyone's harvest.\n\n“Easy fix,” I said.\n\n“It is easy only because Mina questioned it,” Iroh replied.\n\nShe was checking the sealed tins for damp leaves. “And because the farmers answered. Don't leave them out.”\n\nHe inclined his head. “Nor shall I.”\n\nThe next menu draft listed the actual shortage and price. It did not tell a story about a heroic shopkeeper saving a village. The growers had done their work. We were trying to do ours.\n\n\nMina brought the day's receipts over and helped us compare the cost of the new house blend with the old mountain mint. She'd been fielding customer complaints since morning and had a better idea than either of us about which substitutions people actually liked.\n\n\"We should write the reason for the change on the menu,\" she said. \"Not a sad story about the mountain. Just the supply shortage and the new price.\"\n\nIroh nodded. \"And the growers should be paid what they asked.\"\n\n\"Yes. That should be in the ledger, not in the speech you plan to make.\"\n\nHe looked wounded. I laughed into my sleeve.\n\nWe calculated what it would cost to keep one small tin of the rarer mint for customers who specifically wanted it. The rest of the menu could use local herbs whose sources had been checked. No one suggested sneaking unverified plants into a blend to make it seem luxurious.\n\nIroh wrote the numbers neatly, then looked up at me.\n\n\"Do you mind that our next evening won't be quite so extravagant?\"\n\n\"What did you plan?\"\n\n\"Tea. A walk. Possibly two sweet buns.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible disappointment.\"\n\nHis smile brightened. \"Two very good sweet buns.\"\n\nI reached across the counter to touch his hand. We had spoken openly about how I preferred polyamory and practiced ethical non-monogamy. A chosen evening together didn't become less intimate because I could love other people, or because the expensive mint had become scarce.\n\n\"Then I would like your company more than the rare tea,\" I said.\n\n\"An excellent investment.\"\n\nHe leaned close and waited. I kissed him briefly, then we returned to the till before Mina could accuse us of holding up the accounts. There were growers to pay and tomorrow's menu to prepare. This particular romance came with receipts.\n\nAfter closing, I helped Iroh count the till. He checked each coin twice, separated payment owed to workers from money needed for stock, and put a small sum in the repair reserve.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"I was remembering an old habit.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Thinking generosity meant giving something beautiful without asking what it cost.\"\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think generosity sometimes means saying the price aloud and making sure the people who grow the tea can eat.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a lesson worth keeping.\"\n\nHe closed the ledger.\n\n\"Would you like to taste the final blend? I changed the citrus balance.\"\n\n\"Is that an invitation or a request for a quality-control volunteer?\"\n\n\"Both. I am very efficient.\"\n\nWe brewed the last test cup together.\n\nIt tasted of toasted grain, orange peel, and a touch of wild rosemary. It was not the expensive tea. It was good on its own terms.\n\n\"I like it,\" I said.\n\n\"You needn't be kind.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen he pulled a small paper packet from his sleeve.\n\n\"One thing the supplier included.\"\n\nInside was a handful of dried mountain mint, no more than enough for a private pot.\n\n\"They sent a gift?\"\n\n\"A thank-you for accepting the reduced order.\"\n\nI touched the edge of the packet.\n\n\"Are you going to save it for someone important?\"\n\n\"I had thought of sharing it with someone important.\"\n\nI met his eyes.\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it, then looked suddenly shy in a way that still surprised me after all our time together.\n\n\"Tomorrow evening?\" he asked.\n\n\"I would love that.\"\n\nHe tucked the packet away again. We walked home past the lanterns, with the new menu posted and the accounts balanced as far as they could be balanced.\n\nThe next day, a farmer from the supplier's district sent a brief note thanking the teahouse for paying the agreed price. It wasn't a blessing from the spirits or a sudden end to the shortage.\n\nIt was proof that someone on the other side of the road had been treated fairly.\n\nIroh placed the note beside the ledger.\n\nWhen the kettle boiled that evening, he opened the small packet.\n\nWe shared the mint without pretending it had cost nothing.","word_count":1608}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-08":{"number":8,"title":"The Kiss That Lifted Me","content":"For three weeks after dinner, nobody kissed anybody new.\n\nThis was, I learned, entirely intentional.\n\nKatara called it giving everyone room.\n\nAang called it taking things slowly.\n\nToph called it “the dumbest group decision we’ve ever made,” which was interesting because she had voted for it.\n\nThe result was unbearable.\n\nAang started finding excuses to stand close to me.\n\nKatara’s hand lingered at my back when she passed behind my chair.\n\nToph became even more shameless about announcing my pulse.\n\nI started spending unreasonable amounts of time thinking about mouths.\n\nThe breaking point came at a community feast.\n\nCranefish Town had completed the eastern footbridge and the council, desperate for good publicity, decided this deserved lanterns, speeches, food stalls, and a stage. I had been assigned to verify vendor permits. Aang had been assigned to give a speech.\n\nToph had assigned herself to stealing dumplings.\n\nKatara found me near the river just before sunset.\n\n“You look like you’re planning an escape.”\n\n“I am calculating whether anyone would notice if I fell in.”\n\n“I would.”\n\nHer answer was immediate.\n\nI looked at her.\n\nKatara’s smile was soft but not shy.\n\n“Also,” she added, “Toph would complain about having to fish you out.”\n\n“I thought water was your department.”\n\n“She’d complain anyway.”\n\nWe walked toward the lanterns together.\n\nThe feast was loud, crowded, and beautiful. Earth Kingdom drums answered Fire Nation strings. Children ran across the new bridge with paper lanterns. An old woman sold seaweed buns beside a man grilling spicy peppers. Someone had hung four colors of ribbon from every railing.\n\nAang took the stage.\n\nHis speech lasted four minutes.\n\nThis was a miracle.\n\nHe talked about bridges being useful because they did not erase the river. They acknowledged distance and made crossing possible.\n\nIt was a very Aang metaphor.\n\nIt also worked.\n\nWhen he finished, the crowd applauded. His eyes found us almost immediately.\n\nKatara raised one hand.\n\nToph, beside her, shouted, “Too long!”\n\nAang laughed from the stage.\n\nLater, after the official part ended, musicians took over.\n\nAang loved dancing.\n\nThis was less a hobby than a physical law.\n\nHe pulled Katara into the crowd first. She protested for exactly ten seconds before laughing and following his lead. Their movements had the ease of people who had danced badly together often enough to become good at it.\n\nToph stood beside me with her arms folded.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\n“I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“You were about to ask if I dance.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“No.”\n\nA beat.\n\n“Unless there’s stomping.”\n\n“Convenient.”\n\nThe next song had heavy drums.\n\nToph grabbed my wrist.\n\nI barely had time to laugh before she dragged me into the crowd.\n\nToph did not dance like Katara.\n\nShe fought the music.\n\nEvery beat became something to hit with her heel. She moved with sharp confidence, reading the floor through dozens of feet. I stopped trying to follow and let myself answer instead.\n\nWhen I missed a step, she grinned.\n\nWhen I recovered, she grinned wider.\n\nAang and Katara joined us.\n\nFor one song, the four of us moved in a loose circle beneath the lanterns.\n\nNobody belonged to anybody.\n\nNobody was left out.\n\nIt was only dancing.\n\nIt felt like practice for something larger.\n\nThen Aang stole me.\n\nHe spun between Toph and me, caught my hand, and pulled me toward the edge of the crowd.\n\n“Thief!” Toph yelled.\n\n“You can have them back!” Aang called.\n\n“That is not how this works,” Katara shouted, laughing.\n\nAang looked at me. “Is that okay?”\n\nThe question, asked even in play, mattered.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe danced badly.\n\nAang tried to teach me an Air Nomad step involving more hopping than dignity. I stepped on his foot. He nearly backed into a lantern pole. By the end of the song we were laughing too hard to continue.\n\nThe ceremony had gone wrong before we got that far.\n\nThe council chairman called the footbridge complete and invited Aang to cut the ribbon. Aang was halfway through an enthusiastic sentence about the view when Toph stamped her bare heel against the planks.\n\n“Hold up. Who signed off on the south rail?”\n\nThe crowd quieted. The carpenter beside the ribbon flushed and told her the work had been approved that morning.\n\n“By somebody standing on it,” Toph replied, “or by somebody standing behind a desk?”\n\nKatara put down the stack of clinic pamphlets she'd been distributing. She asked the carpenter whether the railing would bear the weight of a person who needed to lean on it. He hesitated. Aang looked from the children waiting beside the bridge to the decorated ribbon.\n\n“We told everyone they could cross,” he said.\n\n“That doesn't make the railing stronger,” Katara replied.\n\nI saw his shoulders stiffen, not at her criticism but at the faces watching him. People had given the Avatar a ribbon and expected the problem to be finished by his smile.\n\nHe lowered the scissors.\n\n“Let's move the feast to this side tonight,” he said. “We'll open the crossing when the people who built it say it's safe.”\n\nA few merchants protested. Toph ignored them and asked the carpenter to fetch his test weights. Katara organized a temporary route to the next safe bridge, making sure the clinic cart could use it. I moved the vendor permits to the new entrance. The chairman called it embarrassing. Nobody argued.\n\nIt was more embarrassing to realize how close we'd come to letting a speech stand in for an inspection.\n\nAang found me by the river afterward, a strip of unused ribbon wound around his wrist.\n\n“I thought I was giving the town something to celebrate,” he said.\n\n“You were. The feast's still happening.”\n\n“Without the bridge.”\n\n“Without someone getting hurt on the bridge.”\n\nHe looked toward Toph, who was explaining the difference between a load test and a wish to three increasingly horrified officials. Aang's mouth twitched.\n\n“She's having fun.”\n\n“She's furious.”\n\n“With Toph, those can coexist.”\n\nHe untied the ribbon and slipped it into his pocket. When we went to the grass, the private things we wanted were still ours to ask for. The public thing was still waiting for a safe answer.\n\n\nWe escaped to the grass beyond the lights.\n\nThe river moved dark beside us.\n\nMusic carried from the feast, softened by distance.\n\nAang sat cross-legged.\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nFor a moment neither of us spoke.\n\nThen he said, “I’ve been trying very hard not to kiss you.”\n\nMy entire body went still.\n\n“That is a terrible opening line.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re supposed to be wise.”\n\n“I’m twenty-one.”\n\n“That is the wisest thing you’ve said tonight.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThen his expression changed.\n\n“I mean it, though.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I don’t want to rush you because we had one conversation.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I also don’t want taking it slow to become pretending I don’t want things.”\n\nThe honesty in his face made my chest ache.\n\n“What do you want?” I asked.\n\nAang’s eyes lowered to my mouth.\n\n“You know.”\n\n“Say it.”\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n“I want to kiss you.”\n\nThe world narrowed.\n\nNot because he was the Avatar.\n\nNot because air moved when he breathed.\n\nBecause he had asked.\n\nBecause he was waiting.\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nAang’s hand lifted slowly to my jaw.\n\nHis palm was warm.\n\nThe first touch of his mouth was gentle.\n\nAlmost careful.\n\nThen I smiled against him, and he did too, and whatever nervousness had been keeping us polite broke apart.\n\nThe kiss deepened.\n\nNot desperate the way I had once imagined it might be. Hungry, yes, but full of laughter we were both trying not to ruin. His thumb brushed my cheek. My hand caught the front of his robe. He made a soft sound that sent warmth through me from throat to stomach.\n\nThen the grass disappeared.\n\nMy eyes opened.\n\nWe were floating.\n\nOnly a foot above the ground, but definitely floating.\n\n“Aang.”\n\nHis eyes opened too.\n\n“Oh.”\n\n“Are you doing this?”\n\n“Not on purpose.”\n\nI started laughing.\n\nThat made him laugh.\n\nThe air current wobble became worse.\n\n“Aang.”\n\n“I know, I know.”\n\nWe drifted sideways.\n\nI grabbed his shoulders.\n\nHe wrapped both arms around me.\n\nWe landed in a heap.\n\nFor several seconds neither of us could breathe from laughing.\n\nFrom somewhere behind us came Toph’s voice.\n\n“Called it.”\n\nWe both froze.\n\nKatara said, “You said you weren’t listening.”\n\n“I wasn’t. They floated.”\n\nI covered my face.\n\nAang called, “How long have you been there?”\n\n“Long enough,” Toph said.\n\n“Not that long,” Katara added quickly.\n\nI sat up.\n\nThey were standing near the lantern line.\n\nKatara’s expression held amusement, tenderness, and something else I could not read.\n\nI stood.\n\nThe old reflex to feel guilty rose before I could stop it.\n\nKatara crossed the distance between us.\n\nShe touched my arm.\n\n“Hey.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\n“You don’t have to look sorry.”\n\n“I’m not.”\n\nToph snorted.\n\nI sighed. “Fine. I was for half a second.”\n\nKatara’s fingers tightened gently around my arm.\n\n“Aang kissing you doesn’t take anything from me.”\n\nAang came up beside us. “And Katara loving you wouldn’t take anything from me.”\n\nToph folded her arms. “And if anybody starts keeping score, I’m eating the scorecard.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nKatara’s gaze moved to my mouth again.\n\nMy pulse jumped.\n\nToph groaned. “Oh, spirits. Here we go.”\n\nKatara smiled.\n\n“Not tonight,” she said, though she did not look away.\n\nThe anticipation in those two words was almost worse than a kiss.\n\nAang slipped his fingers through mine.\n\nKatara took my other hand.\n\nToph complained that hand-holding was structurally inefficient, then hooked two fingers into the back of my sleeve as we walked toward the feast.\n\nThe lanterns glowed over the bridge.\n\nMusic rose.\n\nMy lips still tingled.\n\nFor once, I did not ask myself what any of it meant.\n\nI already knew enough.\n\nI had been kissed.\n\nI had been caught.\n\nAnd when the ground disappeared, three people had been waiting for me to come back down.\n\nThe next morning, Aang apologized for lifting me off the ground.\n\nThis was complicated by the fact that I had enjoyed it.\n\nWe sat on the back steps while Katara argued with a laundry line and Toph loudly claimed not to be listening from the courtyard.\n\n“I should have asked,” Aang said.\n\n“You were kissing me.”\n\n“That is not the same as permission to airbend you.”\n\nThe seriousness in his voice made me stop trying to rescue him from the discomfort.\n\n“No,” I said. “It isn’t.”\n\nHe nodded once.\n\n“I wasn’t frightened after I realized what was happening,” I added. “But I was surprised.”\n\n“I felt happy and my bending just—” He made a helpless motion with both hands. “That’s not an excuse.”\n\n“No.”\n\nAnother nod.\n\nThen I smiled. “It was also extremely dramatic.”\n\nHis ears turned red.\n\nFrom the yard, Toph shouted, “It was ridiculous!”\n\n“You said you weren’t listening,” Aang called.\n\n“I lied.”\n\nKatara emerged carrying a basket of wet clothes. “For what it’s worth, you two floating three feet off the ground while Toph screamed commentary was not the romantic masterpiece either of you thinks it was.”\n\n“I think it was a masterpiece,” I said.\n\nAang looked at me hopefully.\n\n“A terrible one.”\n\nHe laughed, relief loosening his shoulders.\n\nThen he offered his hand palm-up between us.\n\n“Can I?”\n\nI placed mine in it.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nA breeze curled around our joined fingers—small, controlled, no stronger than the air through an open window.\n\nThis time I felt it before it moved.\n\nThis time I chose to stay inside it.\n","word_count":1921},"Love-And-Duty-18":{"number":18,"title":"Roots and Wings","content":"The letter arrived in a cream envelope thick enough to imply money.\n\nI distrusted it immediately.\n\nToph distrusted all envelopes on principle.\n\nKatara distrusted the seal.\n\nAang distrusted nothing until he read the first page.\n\nThen he went very still.\n\nWe were eating breakfast in the courtyard. Toph had one foot hooked around the leg of my chair. Katara was sorting clinic appointment slips. I was pretending not to sort them for her. Aang had been halfway through a steamed bun when the messenger arrived.\n\n“What?” Katara asked.\n\nHe handed her the letter.\n\nShe read.\n\nHer mouth flattened.\n\nToph held out a hand. “Somebody translate the rich-person threat paper for the blind woman.”\n\nI took the second page.\n\nIt was not exactly a threat.\n\nThat almost made it worse.\n\nA coalition of donors supporting Air Nomad cultural restoration wanted to fund a new temple complex outside Cranefish Town. Housing. Archives. Schools. Gardens. A sanctuary for sky bison.\n\nAll good things.\n\nThen came the conditions.\n\nThe board wanted Aang to appoint a hereditary council to “ensure the continuity of authentic Air Nomad bloodlines.” It suggested that his future children should hold ceremonial authority and that “appropriate household arrangements” ought to be considered in light of his responsibility as the last surviving Air Nomad.\n\nThe letter never named Katara.\n\nIt did not name Toph.\n\nIt definitely did not name me.\n\nIt treated us as variables surrounding Aang’s body.\n\nFor several seconds, nobody spoke.\n\nThen Toph said, “I can eat it.”\n\nKatara looked at her.\n\n“The letter,” Toph clarified. “Not the donors. Probably.”\n\nAang stood.\n\n“I need air.”\n\nNobody pointed out that air was everywhere.\n\nHe left through the gate.\n\nKatara started after him.\n\nThen stopped.\n\nHer hand closed into a fist at her side.\n\n“I hate this.”\n\n“So does he,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No. I mean he hates being followed when he needs to think.”\n\nKatara looked toward the gate.\n\nYears of loving someone had taught her when care meant pursuit and when it meant waiting.\n\nShe sat back down.\n\nToph reached for the letter.\n\nI gave it to her.\n\nShe tore it exactly in half.\n\nKatara said, “We may need that.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Evidence.”\n\nToph sighed and handed me the pieces. “Paperwork can heal it.”\n\n“I am not a waterbender for documents.”\n\n“You kind of are.”\n\nI spent the morning angry at people I had never met.\n\nNot because they cared about Air Nomad survival.\n\nAang cared about it more than anyone alive.\n\nBecause they had turned that grief into a claim.\n\nA claim on his future. His children. Our relationships. Katara’s body, perhaps, though they were polite enough not to write that part down.\n\nAt noon, Aang returned.\n\nHis face was calm.\n\nThat did not mean he was calm.\n\n“I’m going to meet them,” he said.\n\nKatara stood. “Not alone.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe looked at all three of us.\n\n“I want you there.”\n\nToph cracked her knuckles.\n\n“Emotionally,” Aang added.\n\n“No promises.”\n\nThe donors received us in a rented council chamber three days later.\n\nSix people sat behind a long table. Two were Air Acolytes. Three were wealthy merchants. One was an Earth Kingdom scholar who had written extensively about Air Nomad history and, judging from Aang’s expression, had occasionally explained that history to Aang himself.\n\nBad choice.\n\nThe chairman began warmly.\n\n“Avatar Aang, our only wish is preservation.”\n\nAang folded his hands.\n\n“Mine too.”\n\nEncouraged, the man continued. “Then you understand why continuity requires planning. Your generation carries obligations no ordinary household would bear.”\n\nToph’s foot tapped once against the stone floor.\n\nKatara spoke before she could.\n\n“Our household is not yours to plan.”\n\nThe chairman smiled in the careful way people did when they wanted to dismiss Katara without appearing to dismiss a war hero.\n\n“Master Katara, no disrespect is intended.”\n\n“Then respect us.”\n\nI loved her a little painfully in that moment.\n\nA scholar cleared his throat. “Historically, communal family structures among Air Nomads—”\n\nAang lifted one hand.\n\nThe room stopped.\n\n“Historically,” he said, “children were raised communally in temples. Air Nomads did not build dynasties.”\n\nThe scholar blinked.\n\nAang’s voice stayed gentle.\n\n“My people valued freedom from attachment to status and possession. You are proposing that my children inherit authority because of blood.”\n\n“We are proposing stability.”\n\n“You are proposing a monarchy with arrows.”\n\nToph made a choking sound beside me.\n\nKatara looked down to hide a smile.\n\nThe chairman’s face reddened.\n\nAang did not.\n\nThat was one of the ways people underestimated him. They mistook kindness for softness. They mistook refusal to dominate for inability to stand firm.\n\nToph had taught him earthbending years ago.\n\nSometimes I could still see the lesson in him.\n\nHe planted himself.\n\n“If I have children,” Aang said, “they will be people. Not cultural property.”\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\n“If Katara has children, if Toph has children, if my partner has children, if any of us do or do not have children, those choices belong to the people making them.”\n\nHe did not look at me while he said it.\n\nI was grateful.\n\nThe room suddenly felt too aware of bodies and possibilities.\n\nOne donor leaned forward. “Avatar, surely you feel some obligation to restore what was lost.”\n\nAang’s expression changed.\n\nFor the first time, anger showed.\n\n“I was twelve when my people were murdered.”\n\nNo one moved.\n\n“I woke up and everyone I had ever known was dead. I have spent every year since then being told I owe the world a solution to that.”\n\nHis voice did not rise.\n\nIt became quieter.\n\n“There is no solution.”\n\nThe words were terrible because they were true.\n\n“I can protect what remains. I can teach. I can rebuild traditions honestly. I can support Air Acolytes. I can protect sacred places. I can raise children, if I choose to have them, with knowledge of where they come from.”\n\nHis hands flattened against the table.\n\n“But I cannot make genocide unhappen by producing descendants.”\n\nKatara’s eyes shone.\n\nToph’s jaw tightened.\n\nI wanted to touch him.\n\nI did not.\n\nThis was his ground to hold.\n\nAang looked at the proposed contract in front of him.\n\n“I will accept funds for a school, archives, gardens, and bison sanctuary with public oversight and no hereditary authority.”\n\nThe chairman inhaled.\n\nAang continued. “Or I will build it slowly without you.”\n\nThat was the end of the meeting.\n\nNot officially.\n\nThere were another forty minutes of negotiation.\n\nBut it was the end in every way that mattered.\n\nOutside, Toph waited until the doors closed.\n\nThen she punched Aang lightly in the shoulder.\n\n“Ow.”\n\n“That was for ‘monarchy with arrows.’”\n\n“You liked that.”\n\n“I loved it. That’s why you get hit.”\n\nKatara wrapped both arms around him.\n\nThis time he let her.\n\nHis face disappeared into her shoulder.\n\nI stood near them, suddenly unsure.\n\nAang reached one hand out without looking.\n\nI took it.\n\nToph pressed her shoulder against his back.\n\nFour people in a government hallway, holding one another badly.\n\nAfter a while, Aang pulled away.\n\n“I meant what I said.”\n\nKatara brushed at the corner of one eye. “About what?”\n\n“Children.”\n\nThe air shifted.\n\nToph groaned. “Can we not turn trauma into a family-planning meeting?”\n\nAang laughed, shaky and grateful.\n\n“No. Not now.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“I just don’t ever want you to think I see you as a possibility for something I need from your body.”\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\nThe rough draft of us—the one I had carried privately before reality made it better—had contained exactly that fear. That I might be valuable because I was a nonbender, because I represented some chance, some future, some answer to a loss too large for any family to repair.\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nToph tilted her head. “For the record, anyone who tries to assign reproductive duties in our house is getting launched into the canal.”\n\nKatara said, “Toph.”\n\n“What? I didn’t say from how high.”\n\nAang laughed again.\n\nThis time, so did I.\n\nThe donors left their proposal on the table, along with a polite warning that the funds would expire if the conditions were not accepted by the next moon.\n\nToph picked up the thick envelope and weighed it in her hand. “They've spent more on paper than the school has on chalk.”\n\nKatara opened the clinic account book. “We can't just say the temple doesn't need money. We have students who need roofs and teachers who need wages.”\n\nAang turned away from the window. “I don't want our people priced into somebody else's rules.”\n\n“Then don't let the answer end at no,” she told him. “Who else would help?”\n\nThat was the difficult part. The board had offered certainty, which was almost irresistible to a man trying to preserve a culture after a genocide. We spent the afternoon asking less impressive questions. Could the community kitchen contribute meals one day a week? Could the builders donate work if the school purchased its own materials? Would the council allow cultural lessons in an existing meeting room until construction was affordable?\n\nAang wanted to send a message to every surviving Air Nomad artifact collector at once. I asked him to start with the people who might actually want a class, not merely a monument. He stopped, then smiled ruefully.\n\n“I sound like the chairman,” he said.\n\n“No,” Toph replied. “The chairman would've charged you for the envelope.”\n\nAang laughed, but the sound disappeared when Katara counted the costs. We could open one temporary classroom, not a temple. It would need a safe stove and a teacher with a schedule.\n\n“I know someone who can teach the children the old songs,” Aang said.\n\n“Ask them,” Katara answered.\n\n“I will.”\n\nHe wrote the message himself. I watched him cross out *we need you* and replace it with *would you be willing?* There was history in every word he chose; there was also a living person on the other end of the letter.\n\n\nThat night we climbed onto our roof.\n\nAang lay flat on his back, looking at stars none of us could see the same way.\n\nKatara rested against his shoulder. Toph lay with one foot touching mine. I watched clouds move over the moon.\n\n“Roots and wings,” Aang said.\n\nToph groaned. “Here comes the poetry.”\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“That’s worse.”\n\nAang ignored her.\n\n“I used to think rebuilding meant making things the way they were.”\n\nKatara traced a circle against his sleeve.\n\n“And now?” she asked.\n\n“Now I think roots are what tell you where you came from.” He smiled faintly. “Wings are what let you become something your ancestors didn’t have to imagine.”\n\nToph was quiet.\n\nThen: “Still poetry.”\n\n“Good poetry?”\n\n“No.”\n\nI reached across the roof and found his hand in the dark.\n\nHe did not need to restore an extinct world by turning us into instruments of it.\n\nWe could help him remember.\n\nWe could help him build.\n\nWe could love the living man without demanding that he become a monument to the dead.\n\nFor that night, it was enough.\n","word_count":1839},"love-and-duty-42":{"number":42,"title":"The Shape of Fair Time","content":"Our family scheduling argument began with a square drawn in blue ink.\n\nIt was supposed to represent Tuesday.\n\nKatara had divided the page into days, with each day divided into morning, afternoon, and evening. She had written clinic shifts in neat columns. Aang's council meetings occupied the top half of the week. Toph's bridge inspections ran along the bottom in a handwriting large enough to be considered structural reinforcement.\n\nMy own commitments were squeezed into the margins.\n\n\"That's not Tuesday,\" I said.\n\nKatara examined the square.\n\n\"It is literally Tuesday.\"\n\n\"It looks like a punishment.\"\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Give me the calendar. I'll earthbend us a new week.\"\n\n\"We cannot bend time,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Then why are you the Avatar?\"\n\nHe gave her the patient look he used when he was trying very hard to remember that he loved her.\n\nI had agreed to the meeting because the previous week had been a mess. I was supposed to help Iroh at the pantry on Wednesday, check the supply ledger at Brasswatch on Thursday, and spend Friday evening with Katara, just the two of us. Instead, the bridge inspection ran late, Danse needed an additional sample, and I forgot to tell Katara that I'd promised to review a different set of measurements.\n\nBy the time I arrived home Friday, there were two cold bowls on the table and no one in the kitchen.\n\nKatara hadn't shouted.\n\nThat was worse.\n\nNow she sat across from me with her hands folded, waiting.\n\n\"Tell me what bothered you,\" I said.\n\n\"I waited,\" she answered. \"Then I stopped waiting because I had an early shift. I don't mind that you help people. I mind finding out you won't be home from a note you leave after I've already made plans.\"\n\nI stared at the calendar.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nAang looked relieved.\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"That was fast. Usually people spend an hour defending themselves first.\"\n\n\"I can still do that if you want entertainment.\"\n\n\"No thanks.\"\n\nKatara looked at me, not smiling.\n\n\"I know our relationships are complicated. I know we agreed we weren't going to measure affection by equal hours. But when we make plans, I need to be able to trust them.\"\n\n\"I agree.\"\n\nAang cleared his throat.\n\n\"I think I've done something similar.\"\n\nToph turned toward him.\n\n\"Only the part where you promise you'll be home by sundown and then spend three hours negotiating with a council because you don't want anybody upset.\"\n\nAang winced.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\n\"You're all painfully reasonable today.\"\n\n\"Give us time,\" I said.\n\nThe difficulty wasn't that we had too many people to love. It was that we kept mistaking good intentions for a working system. Nobody wanted to appear selfish. So we waited until the disappointment hurt enough to become an argument.\n\nKatara pushed the blue square toward me.\n\n\"Tell me where you actually need time this week.\"\n\n\"Monday evening with you, if you want it. Wednesday I need a quiet night by myself. Thursday I'm with Iroh for the pantry inventory. Saturday I promised Greg I'd help set up the pier.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"And when do we spend time together?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Do you mean everyone, or you and me?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I meant everyone. But maybe I want both.\"\n\n\"Then we should write both.\"\n\nToph tapped the edge of the table.\n\n\"And stop treating the whole group as one giant person.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" Katara said.\n\nToph leaned back, pleased with herself.\n\nI wrote separate lines for individual time and group time. It looked less tidy. It was more honest.\n\nWe found out quickly that fairness was not the same as giving everyone an identical share of every week. Katara needed reliable advance notice because of her clinic shifts. Aang wanted space for spontaneous visits without making them obligatory. Toph preferred a whole afternoon of work and teasing to an evening of ceremonious romance. I needed time alone and time for other people I cared about. None of those needs were defects.\n\nThen Toph made a noise like she'd swallowed a nail.\n\n\"What?\" I asked.\n\n\"You've left a space for me on Friday.\"\n\n\"Do you want it?\"\n\n\"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Then it stays open. No obligation.\"\n\nShe turned her head toward me.\n\n\"You don't have to keep a chair for someone who hasn't said yes.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to offer.\"\n\nFor a moment she looked almost shy.\n\n\"Maybe,\" she said.\n\nI nearly fell off my chair.\n\nAang smiled into his tea.\n\nKatara caught him.\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm not doing anything.\"\n\n\"You're glowing.\"\n\n\"Airbender condition.\"\n\nToph threw a napkin at him.\n\nThe calendar looked tidy enough to be suspicious.\n\nBefore I could congratulate us, Toph tapped the corner of the page.\n\n“That's not a calendar,” she said. “That's a trap with very tidy boxes.”\n\nKatara frowned. “We've left free time.”\n\n“Free for whom? My bridge review starts at dawn. If I spend Tuesday night with you, I get four hours of sleep and then somebody asks me why the west supports are crooked.”\n\nAang looked at the row he'd marked for himself. He had set aside three consecutive evenings for council negotiations. He hadn't included the time he'd need to prepare.\n\n“I thought I could finish early,” he said.\n\nToph made a derisive noise. “I thought I could teach the entire city to earthbend by lunchtime. We're both disappointed.”\n\nI moved my own commitments out of the margin and onto a separate sheet. One belonged to Greg in Beach City, another to Danse at Brasswatch, another to a quiet evening I wanted entirely alone. The fact that Jasper was the person who traveled between the doors did not make the whole set of obligations flexible.\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous,” I told them, “and I prefer polyamory. You know that. But a calendar doesn't turn all the people I love into one giant relationship with interchangeable hours.”\n\nKatara nodded. “And being together doesn't mean we stop needing time apart.”\n\nAang took the brush. “Then we should put *rest* on the page first.”\n\n“And no assigning *my* rest,” Toph added.\n\nShe drummed her heel against the floorboards while I marked my writing time. I was surprised at how relieved I felt seeing an empty square stay empty. Nobody filled it with a favor.\n\nWhen the final version was ready, we agreed to try it for a week. A schedule could be an invitation to keep promises; it was not permission to make them for someone else.\n\n\nI counted the squares once more, then realized I'd put three people's needs on Wednesday evening and left Thursday almost empty. It had seemed fair when I was filling in boxes. It looked less fair when I imagined actual people trying to get through a door.\n\n\"Can we test one week before calling this finished?\" I asked.\n\nToph leaned back. \"The schedule has homework now?\"\n\n\"It always did,\" Katara said.\n\nAang agreed to handle Appa's morning care on the days Katara had clinic appointments. Toph wanted the bridge review kept separate from household chores, because having a gift for stone didn't mean she was available to fix every broken shelf. I wrote down my writing time and asked everyone not to treat it as an empty square simply because I would be home.\n\n\"How do we tell when somebody's overwhelmed?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Ask them,\" Toph said. \"Revolutionary concept.\"\n\nKatara laughed. \"And believe the answer.\"\n\nWe tried a mock change: a clinic message arriving during a planned family dinner. Katara would decide whether she needed to go. Aang would check whether transport was required without volunteering Appa before discussing the return. Toph would not be drafted to babysit anyone's emotional reaction, and I would be allowed to say I was disappointed without making the disappointment an argument.\n\nThe first attempt was awful. We all talked at once.\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said, \"we should practice one person speaking at a time.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Not a chance.\"\n\nBut she waited for me to finish. Aang went next, then Katara. The conversation took longer, and everyone understood more of it.\n\nI thought about how long it had taken us to name the relationship as polyamorous, about telling each partner plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred love that could be shared honestly. A calendar couldn't do the loving for us. It could stop ordinary mistakes from becoming proof that somebody was unwanted.\n\nBy supper, the plan was imperfect but usable. Katara leaned against my shoulder while Aang cleared the table. Toph stole the last dumpling and claimed it was part of the test.\n\n\"Of what?\" I asked.\n\n\"Whether you can survive disappointment.\"\n\n\"Apparently I can.\"\n\nShe smiled in the particular way that warned me she was pleased, then kicked my chair gently under the table. It was nothing like a formal vow. It felt more like a family.\n\nWe finished the calendar in an hour, which was astonishing considering how often Toph tried to rename Wednesday. We made one rule I cared about particularly: changing a plan meant actually telling the people affected. No silent assumptions that a loving partner would understand without being asked.\n\nThen we discovered the missing problem.\n\nThe house had only one quiet room.\n\nAang used it to meditate. Katara used it to prepare clinic records. Toph went there when too much noise made it hard to concentrate on what she felt through the floor. I went there to write.\n\nAll four of us had been politely avoiding it when someone else was inside.\n\n\"Which means,\" I said, \"we need to talk about the room as seriously as the relationship.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally. A structural problem.\"\n\nWe spent the afternoon moving furniture. Aang made a low screen from cloth and light wood. Katara rearranged the shelves so medicines stayed in the proper light. Toph checked that the chairs could be moved without blocking the door. I measured the table twice and still got one dimension wrong.\n\nToph corrected me.\n\n\"You're terrible at lying to a ruler.\"\n\n\"I didn't lie.\"\n\n\"Your ruler did.\"\n\nBy evening the room had two workable areas and a sign that could be turned to say PLEASE KNOCK. It did not solve every issue, but it meant needing privacy didn't require giving a speech.\n\nKatara found me there after dinner.\n\nShe tapped on the frame.\n\n\"Can I come in?\"\n\nI turned the sign over.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nShe entered carrying two cups of tea and a small plate of dumplings. She set them down, then stood beside the table.\n\n\"I was still angry this morning,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm less angry now.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not because we drew squares.\"\n\n\"That was my favorite part.\"\n\nShe laughed softly.\n\n\"Because you listened. And because you didn't promise you'll never disappoint me again.\"\n\n\"I probably will.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\nI stood. Her hands were warm when I took them.\n\n\"I'm sorry I left you waiting,\" I said. \"I will call or send word before the plan changes next time. Not afterward.\"\n\nShe stepped closer.\n\n\"That's what I needed.\"\n\nThe kiss was quiet at first. She brushed her thumb against my cheek, and I felt the pull of affection and embarrassment together, the strange tenderness of being forgiven without being excused. When I leaned in again, she smiled against my mouth.\n\n\"Monday,\" she murmured.\n\n\"Monday.\"\n\n\"Don't make me put it on a banner.\"\n\n\"Toph would.\"\n\n\"She would spell it wrong on purpose.\"\n\nWe laughed and stayed together until the tea cooled.\n\nThe next morning, Aang added an extra square to the calendar.\n\nIt read NO SCHEDULING DURING BREAKFAST.\n\nToph changed it to NO MEETINGS BEFORE DUMPLINGS.\n\nNobody objected.\n\nIt was the first schedule we'd made that felt less like a test and more like a way to give our choices somewhere to live.","word_count":1956}}};for(const s of lib.series){const dict=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const update=dict[ch.id];if(update&&update.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.content=update.content;ch.title=update.title;ch.word_count=update.word_count;}}}})();
